#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a53314f-f55e-5de3-9361-255122ecd28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476251} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7ed870-0b8d-550d-abc6-3a5002277034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476272} {:answer/id #uuid "20349e86-f87d-5afc-84ce-b2382a2a6350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476276} {:answer/id #uuid "59076299-fa05-510b-bc19-29ea93c3586f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476392} {:answer/id #uuid "9c74adee-37f0-5906-921a-264e40407fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9146ede4-3856-5d3a-8193-cdcf33cf5f1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "74d58075-0369-56a0-b633-ba74268879be" :question/math-academy-id "q-142916" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{9}, & 0\\le x\\le 9,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{9}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{x=0}^{x=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{27}{[{x}^{3/2}]}_{x=0}^{x=9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{27}({9}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{27}(27-0) \\\\  & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ {y}^{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)={y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a60ab7a-885a-5193-ae56-9d4015f6021d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476252} {:answer/id #uuid "4c210ddc-4c61-57c7-aa39-2daf86a46ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476359} {:answer/id #uuid "9775a468-23a5-5daa-804f-ac36de0d6f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476532} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bcef07-e2f6-5e8d-8cfe-04807b4326f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476560} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f5661-0f83-5055-8b00-f0f80aa42f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476532} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ade0a2-d2b7-5d69-add8-6955d332e25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a9fb34d-c302-5432-923d-53ca78af43c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143836" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy,\\, & 0\\le x,\\,0\\le y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/df/dfa15482cfa0e8fdc9e98b05a71d2b64a89b3886da44f6966fee9f1b5e096eef.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}x{y}^{2}\\cdot 8xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{105}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5351eae7-dc6c-5a57-81bb-251115e032a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476378} {:answer/id #uuid "63acac21-1914-55df-8f46-e1df0c572d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476429} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5cfbf8-b34e-5c08-942c-fd1b4c89d51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476443} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7225b6-8f8a-507a-869b-7e8fc526e349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476590} {:answer/id #uuid "fbad6de2-e944-5d9d-8691-ad17c4fe0fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476378} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc25465-db53-5da9-ab78-ec9a5725f2c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1209ac5-9324-5c4f-8a14-c5d815ce9e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-142888" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot y)-(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}+0\\cdot y)] \\\\  & =y+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17aadaf2-4ba7-5796-a001-c0bbe9dcac22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476242} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8a0898-7457-5d87-af4d-85f0fb6d7665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x+1}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476338} {:answer/id #uuid "9f32af68-e5ee-55a4-9cfe-8d1f23989236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476546} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7ae9e-7458-5741-9279-66e4924e7c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{9}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476638} {:answer/id #uuid "c6feae73-dcc8-57f0-b4da-622fffd1cf65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476651} :answer-field/id #uuid "b821f73d-7070-57a0-ab5a-2ac12f5bc93f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "acc3ecee-2ca3-52a4-a564-fe35d9f4b095" :question/math-academy-id "q-142754" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{9}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}(2)+\\frac{4}{9}x\\cdot {2}^{2})-({x}^{2}(1)+\\frac{4}{9}x\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72270fd2-6992-5f31-97a2-2ca4ee17ded5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{25}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476461} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced4ceb-454c-52c6-af4d-6e2ad83dfe18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{25}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476480} {:answer/id #uuid "968b508d-00d7-5293-968a-76a5988b5ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476531} {:answer/id #uuid "a976e357-0ba9-5d47-9790-62cdf8d6dbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{25}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476576} {:answer/id #uuid "b606dc72-4cfc-5e8f-9fe7-991ba2e0b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476461} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b857419-f18b-50b2-bb98-09131889cb3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aecec449-e3ae-5803-8a70-f99f6be91bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-142911" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 5).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-5{e}^{-(5x+\\frac{y}{5})}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =-5(0-{e}^{-(5x+0)}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}5{e}^{-5x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-25}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{25}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9113dee1-1184-5d4f-aa27-da463ed39bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476514} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d64684-dede-539a-83c4-b43a77b362ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476554} {:answer/id #uuid "af717fad-e100-59bb-b086-82c6e11d1bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476594} {:answer/id #uuid "b762868b-9280-5c3f-8b90-87d5f4955e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476618} {:answer/id #uuid "ca885fbf-1c70-5e4e-a280-d699b127bab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476554} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68816de-9c55-5f5d-8b88-343571543dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aef0f15c-e4b9-546e-bc50-4ed011233821" :question/math-academy-id "q-97675" :question/problem "A particular traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Each day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August has an equal probability of being chosen. What is the probability that the festival will take place before the $18$th of August next year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the date of the festival. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<18).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and the set $S=\\{16,17,…,25\\}$ has $|S|=25-16+1=10$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & x=16,17,…,25 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<18$ are $16,17.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<18)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd49525-ce7b-5de7-bb4b-853ca8c7b51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}-3{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476308} {:answer/id #uuid "5f91b3ea-874f-54c6-9b5a-27d6749d139c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476417} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2b6b61-00a6-5cd5-b748-9e114f816504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1+{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476504} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd12cd1-d9b6-5434-b061-5a342e650a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1+3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476548} {:answer/id #uuid "d5462deb-905b-5315-b5e7-9ee7d87bb3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3+{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476680} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4e6988-a890-5061-983b-4ccf67591ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b503f4b2-2e6a-599e-9f9e-bfcbf92fe455" :question/math-academy-id "q-142710" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3},\\,0\\le 3y\\le x\\le 3.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 3y\\le x\\le 3\\}.$\n\n![](images/54/54c803be9ecc7873f2624fc9d083997039cb651c11cc38eff6fa561ec064b7a5.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{3y}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{3y}^{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=3y}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}+3\\cdot {y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3y)^{3}-3y\\cdot {y}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[9+3{y}^{2}-12{y}^{3}] \\\\  & =3+{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3+{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e05135-dc72-54d1-a265-7303f0c55750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476249} {:answer/id #uuid "2a48b4c7-e269-5b07-a4ea-37ff96fee042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476299} {:answer/id #uuid "772175ea-626c-579e-9d22-f7b5ba8cba19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476471} {:answer/id #uuid "b8329249-ead1-5e17-95ed-e5b59ee76c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476620} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e5ae14-8d9d-5fd7-be35-533fed06278b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476656} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b5eb91d-c108-5a2b-99e3-c07f16397e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b66f8317-7401-5c42-a057-174f43015e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-142924" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{y=1}^{y=b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[1-\\frac{1}{b}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b78aa06-4999-59c3-8bc9-3a7d872f529f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476302} {:answer/id #uuid "ac565ee3-a0f8-59be-8dcf-53639d60f3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476587} {:answer/id #uuid "d8bdc362-b396-51bc-87a5-023be90d62f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476693} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ae932a-3d14-5a25-b6c5-4f37c85ff36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476707} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c033d5-3563-59aa-a554-4ce06b15d676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476693} :answer-field/id #uuid "d512cc49-4e5a-531f-9fa7-3ee72ee1e793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b80e305c-0467-5dea-919a-dc17751b8238" :question/math-academy-id "q-143885" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}y\\sqrt{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4[-\\frac{1}{y}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320d3cb5-bef1-53b0-95c4-cff9ede43030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476314} {:answer/id #uuid "4af6259e-a55c-5863-973f-a60c2eba3557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476358} {:answer/id #uuid "95ffaac9-33b1-5d66-82ac-051de97f1e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476527} {:answer/id #uuid "bd15e8ea-65f0-52e8-8cf8-066dd559eb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476633} {:answer/id #uuid "f5587a79-ea8a-50f2-8937-9dd583bde64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476743} :answer-field/id #uuid "341d4747-e282-5d0e-9b1a-d8cec6a359cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bdb3668b-11de-51d3-b228-d2e5f99dedeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-142951" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{21}(xy)^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{21}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{63}({2}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596fb15b-9278-5dfe-92e9-2a47c3fb8ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476396} {:answer/id #uuid "66e5a963-fe67-5131-9387-46c125d4871b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476435} {:answer/id #uuid "8199d30b-51f8-5be5-a28a-a65da3d6c2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476487} {:answer/id #uuid "97fc35cf-14d0-5200-a801-d88272cd7dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476536} {:answer/id #uuid "a03baadf-376f-5b6b-b06b-019bdd1343dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476396} :answer-field/id #uuid "290d3510-e616-5c9a-9571-b43c639908e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6513aec-b650-5caf-b4d5-5b3d2bca02b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110866" :question/problem "Let $D=[1,e]\\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-y}{4e},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{xy},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2({e}^{2}-1)y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[({e}^{2}-1){y}^{2}]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,e)=\\frac{2-e}{4e}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6ec337-c662-5c0a-b5e2-69fe04d1ef2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476266} {:answer/id #uuid "3145a0e7-2f04-5b2a-bfd5-a6183dbdca2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476311} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7479a3-2e3b-5382-8307-db0975928c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476341} {:answer/id #uuid "5950bdae-2411-5e41-baa1-59e37045d9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476394} {:answer/id #uuid "856a46b4-906d-57ae-9ba7-0310565509bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476341} :answer-field/id #uuid "d372f530-a615-5f81-b8cf-700c6a8693b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb839c9a-7589-5685-a16a-88269e423380" :question/math-academy-id "q-143875" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15adda06-7460-52b4-aa55-deb6fe78e392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476238} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef6dbeb-2c81-5d67-b792-b5e8d9cb0c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476271} {:answer/id #uuid "3c210a12-56dc-5259-bee4-87f88746343f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476335} {:answer/id #uuid "867fa6aa-682d-5171-baa6-b54dd3c655c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476496} {:answer/id #uuid "ec48e0ed-1384-59b8-bce0-b8f7831f77c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476731} :answer-field/id #uuid "1621230a-953e-5d54-b11b-7c7cd6cf85fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df4b9bd0-fd66-55ed-92b5-c754afe8ffc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143905" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/bc/bc44d4c99cf60148685a99b5c3b5ea2e732ea4b9939aefe5ba465d46e92c5ea8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}\\cdot [y]_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bec6a99-1dac-567c-813b-ca1e14f2ad8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476222} {:answer/id #uuid "268a0b39-0f03-514d-b977-9395434bf5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476291} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5a268f-b171-549b-9622-61eb5dfb7353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476478} {:answer/id #uuid "a16bb00a-4fa3-5b54-a4c4-2b328f72c1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476557} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ac507e-7c62-54c0-8c76-cec9f9d10416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476222} :answer-field/id #uuid "02f7c8e3-76aa-5dcd-975e-d8ba1486379b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e42bb61d-9253-506b-85c5-8f5b23f330ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-143855" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/ff/ff35a5c562607b4ac314d3ae755156d75baf5d8b01b813f6b59d67dac540e154.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}(2-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16baf521-4ad1-5f51-b284-e99c35d1a599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476241} {:answer/id #uuid "224dab25-b7a8-5374-9f30-997a3bc4b3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476280} {:answer/id #uuid "267c7a2e-8020-5c81-8c3a-9b340eeea5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476290} {:answer/id #uuid "6f244e6a-cd06-5795-87e3-d153aff5c444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476451} {:answer/id #uuid "952d1764-5ad0-5545-b93b-d0db05c1cd66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476241} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ca40ef-a595-54c6-929e-f2241c06940d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1049e31-fa99-5f07-ad8f-6d47af518d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-143831" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y,\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{y}{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/54/54777bc840acd1d1c24da08ccb99025b039af9c47421602a3d9dd73df2a91779.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{y}{7x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{7x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{y}{7x}\\cdot \\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16130171-28c9-5e26-847c-56cba78bf71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476239} {:answer/id #uuid "804ae345-3ee0-562b-a4f2-fd882a705b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476486} {:answer/id #uuid "962381dc-a808-5d2b-a4df-151f0ed19e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476529} {:answer/id #uuid "9941cb5d-24d9-55fe-afb0-b8b524fa2de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476537} {:answer/id #uuid "ebecbae9-4e6e-51c2-aa5e-8c7b1ff2b9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476239} :answer-field/id #uuid "eed8ad9e-e0da-5240-bd75-5bd17b2051da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbbae5a7-4143-5b60-9c21-0b9858325bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110867" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c,\\, & 0\\le y<1-x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y<1-x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a4fce220150c53a70ca236843203460a7040cc1a1b5dfe664c6d45dd93ac1.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cy]}_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c(1-x)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b043ff-ed32-5ad4-8ebf-e2dbbeee6aa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477780} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbdc895-0ad3-573a-b041-364e600b7833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{97}{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477967} {:answer/id #uuid "76807ce6-5aeb-5a78-bae9-dad2221d3b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{113}{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477983} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe4e1b2-7e7c-587d-9be9-d85905eba39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478102} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5e3592-112b-5b2a-a863-26e667cdaf7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477780} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a2ff3fa-3979-54c7-9c20-3011a115fa47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0eaad2fb-faea-5de0-88f1-158f02ba672a" :question/math-academy-id "q-149609" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{7}(x+\\sqrt{y}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{7},$ find $\\text{E}[2X+Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6}{7}(x+\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x\\sqrt{y}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}(2\\sqrt{y}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3}{7}(2\\sqrt{y}+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{3/2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}{[\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{5/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}(\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X+Y] & =2\\,\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{39}{70}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{80+39}{70} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3915f74c-160e-5152-b96a-aa964c8e6d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.289" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477842} {:answer/id #uuid "41191433-d83d-5aef-b9ef-6c0eafed2ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477858} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d0fc68-fa7c-5c6f-94a5-1a96c8da2fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.382" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478056} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6e5e8-9170-5a8e-b1b3-f5e944fbbc70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.278" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478183} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d7459-2b62-5aa7-8e9a-444810f04b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478202} :answer-field/id #uuid "543f86a7-9d04-5010-8199-ce0c0b78789a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22625d61-74d2-5702-9e7b-d31919b31ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-143361" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{7}{12}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{24}]}_{1}^{1.5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7(1.5)^{2}}{24}-\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.365.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c3eb513-f277-57b1-a8f8-5d539a198456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477922} {:answer/id #uuid "62969252-729b-558e-a929-ca26c9421d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(x+1){e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477939} {:answer/id #uuid "7af45ab1-82da-5803-966f-cc3a6b56e690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({y}^{2}+1){e}^{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477988} {:answer/id #uuid "7eafcf78-0a55-5493-885a-ef84c05d0caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+1){e}^{2y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477997} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdd4ec2-219e-59c6-aacc-1ad870c4924d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477922} :answer-field/id #uuid "1163d6a2-1bf2-5d52-8be1-3f994d864230" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "321896e4-7ca4-574a-a1b1-f3375d212e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-143351" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d546f32-ef09-541b-97ac-be58cb5dc44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477758} {:answer/id #uuid "45d86a13-2930-5fbe-9f9e-208e3a7528ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477868} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6558e5-634d-558a-88ea-d6cf86b16af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477885} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ee63da-8fcd-5b05-a984-8fe6adf18a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478119} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc6282-6b95-51b0-a9d8-b8431a9541e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478167}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477885} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfc71ab-b965-55c6-8fd6-517e70644c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "418cd51b-54c6-5702-b58c-50620816a1ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-143356" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [2,4]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8+4)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{9}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026d0ea1-9366-5664-b4b9-994b0042a77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477738} {:answer/id #uuid "31b68073-6689-585d-ae4a-b07f5e178bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477827} {:answer/id #uuid "546d128e-cde5-59e1-ab9e-a78e7cc369cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477912} {:answer/id #uuid "72d5367b-b862-518e-8884-551d2a8c05d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477979} {:answer/id #uuid "c20a011c-44e2-5773-889d-7b1fc1a3e892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478114}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f9d110-27a8-57fc-9bdb-1973382778ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "427c86d9-8f85-5921-9341-2ec04eff93eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143358" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}y, & \\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,4]\\times [2,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,2\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{20}xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{20}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4447cc98-6743-5c8b-ae0d-f98d22239d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477863} {:answer/id #uuid "6296064a-90d1-5369-b44b-518fa6f48fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477938} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bdf207-5309-5c13-8d6f-2a82165c7979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478086} {:answer/id #uuid "daf607a2-d3a0-519f-ab73-cf8bd7135eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478164} {:answer/id #uuid "e4406152-e5b4-5362-b7c4-2fdf2a10e810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477938} :answer-field/id #uuid "08f42a5d-307f-5689-a28c-cb44ddd1c806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5aba2c2c-b7e8-572a-a813-c98db854f118" :question/math-academy-id "q-149614" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(x+2y), & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{17}{30},$ find $\\text{E}[X+2Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $1\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{5}(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[xy+{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{2(x+1)}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{8}{3}+2-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+2Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+2\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{15}+2\\left(\\frac{17}{30}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{15}+\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2111ac82-07a6-57fc-92e2-4359e8908bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477797} {:answer/id #uuid "4d64756e-fe2f-54b7-b56f-ba3b17d7bc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477895} {:answer/id #uuid "67ff2263-d24d-5772-b801-d642d3b899df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477945} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb9fa73-242d-54e2-9813-05078b917a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478037} {:answer/id #uuid "e90a54d3-0da8-5502-b810-6932b4f13e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478037} :answer-field/id #uuid "c407a9d0-53e9-57fe-a94b-9719e1488ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ef50eeb-da62-5943-922a-49e7f6ad7489" :question/math-academy-id "q-143343" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e826808-605f-5120-948a-59aa92c12a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477791} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa102a5-c5aa-52f0-989d-f63963d7b079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477794} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b93fcd-32b7-5a8a-ba02-6b06a9ee6a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478061} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f0a92b-fef0-5f9a-987b-f4a0d5aba13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478132} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ba1b0c-7cb3-592b-be9b-86271440aa5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478061} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2390c11-a776-5a6c-9a83-24a35d5a12f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b80fc5d-3cc1-506f-9e31-9f2ad06b5cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143375" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\right)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a206236-0e7e-5bbc-8d9f-f1ab1aa63362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477753} {:answer/id #uuid "147e0940-a76d-5258-82a8-9ce0853f0d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477771} {:answer/id #uuid "69fc415e-381a-5926-85eb-1f8964653882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477950} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0499d6-0793-579a-b3ca-91fa82d7407c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477963} {:answer/id #uuid "7f62311f-2802-59c1-89f9-ddd0175dfae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477753} :answer-field/id #uuid "562abd85-92be-5ffd-a029-9f990daee18d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8523bc2d-4a4c-583b-88c5-0e1cec2367ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-143355" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,0\\le y, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x-y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{b}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06de0808-d75c-5542-8785-2b794c445fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477747} {:answer/id #uuid "51924fc7-2fd0-5328-9944-f6e3cdb40a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477902} {:answer/id #uuid "95c29e5c-b4a7-5d82-979c-afede81345d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478028} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fbbc9c-012e-5a54-abce-85136bc47289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478125} {:answer/id #uuid "c98309c8-400d-5a8e-9816-a2efbfa6aa24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478133} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f0a460-84c7-54c5-b966-be06a0c78634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "85b259be-6bf2-5d1d-9133-ee3e5619e62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143367" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+2), & \\,1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,3]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(2+4)-\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{1}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{5}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12570832-3b6e-5b6a-a287-f07c08e82469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477767} {:answer/id #uuid "49cebb4d-b38a-5cf8-b82c-e24d65ce5841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477881} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd03053-40a1-5247-bf67-f15a373a1271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477993} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f16c4e-2ef7-5efd-a643-9353ecd1c160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478057} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b0f5cc-c808-51bb-83ca-aa8b0db3fc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478182} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d767f87-00d4-53d3-9572-4ca534bc85cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9118031b-d926-55ed-8f7f-1b50c43555cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-143581" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $\\text{E}[5X-Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3}(\\frac{2}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\cdot \\frac{2y}{9}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(9-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-Y] & =5\\,\\text{E}[X]+(-1)\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+(-1)(2) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d389b13-cfd3-5648-b491-bd03bbd0f6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x(y+1)^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477789} {:answer/id #uuid "482bddba-7826-5a7a-9481-6318c2041403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477877} {:answer/id #uuid "613da1a0-2c7c-5b40-8004-729f27c2a407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477933} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc289fe-8cd8-534c-be91-cf8031232756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}(x+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478109} {:answer/id #uuid "d58623b4-ab17-5950-b533-644d2efc520c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478158} :answer-field/id #uuid "179aa336-60c2-5460-a3dc-99b5cf0016ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9efe9c4-330f-5e24-830e-56ad2ba97826" :question/math-academy-id "q-143342" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(y+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}(y+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119315f1-83ce-5a6d-bbc2-c00d428fdecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477764} {:answer/id #uuid "19b24d2b-c4be-5498-9ef9-469fe4b09f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477781} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc8938b-fd54-51cb-aba9-d1d1f78e12d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478041} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e7b647-652d-5261-83d8-0b43e4647da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478151} {:answer/id #uuid "e459d44b-76eb-50a7-9c70-6772fdb63601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478041} :answer-field/id #uuid "847b995d-d2da-58ee-9d30-8b4bd5b80731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b7f5fbd8-1657-5933-82eb-1254d5fe2e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-143380" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(3{x}^{2})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2})}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cb9131c-d9eb-5cef-8e05-9ff2d9b72f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}(y-1)\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477925} {:answer/id #uuid "74029174-1966-5659-83fe-2592772dacda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477980} {:answer/id #uuid "89c7b625-6def-5a2d-8456-aeaab562159a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}(x-1)\\sqrt{2y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478014} {:answer/id #uuid "acad0ea1-c3a9-5b2c-bf4c-e2e36b44ad54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)\\sqrt{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478067} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8d834e-9ed1-5dcb-a3d9-f32dc5876e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}(y-1)\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477980} :answer-field/id #uuid "80fc6390-6a5b-5132-9deb-4b766c3612c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bdd3bac4-4f21-5e93-9c5a-1e5e329b0b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-143345" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2582ad56-e0fc-5e45-a724-0bf543eb47b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477809} {:answer/id #uuid "6d662136-b6b4-51b7-b84c-28e07ae855b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477962} {:answer/id #uuid "926d3769-d90a-5427-ba8d-6e61ed8728c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478024} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a07fa4-4e2c-5f65-b0cc-82609ae32a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478228} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6ba3f-5d68-5219-9887-1a4d3342335a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477962} :answer-field/id #uuid "456ccbaa-8e08-50d2-a659-91ea1be33e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dc0d3274-ca1e-5ab4-bb51-ab607148446e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143580" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find $\\text{E}[X+Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3506dab1-62ad-546f-b8d5-2e43cdacdb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477838} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9ffb8b-392e-547a-b8dd-0ab8af442d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477886} {:answer/id #uuid "91eb4fef-125c-5602-af85-46700f3acf65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478023} {:answer/id #uuid "9b33856e-57bb-575b-a78c-287c2948dabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478034} {:answer/id #uuid "c64ada4d-645e-5dde-adf5-9d7a1b8e974e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478034} :answer-field/id #uuid "754fcbaf-4a94-546a-a360-fa1f3050ad24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e4997f39-9351-5840-b1b4-1804fe6c9f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97971" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $B+3I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2977e316-273e-5445-b50a-cb57603ee29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477813} {:answer/id #uuid "72c2b192-0559-53f5-881a-0579227ab7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477978} {:answer/id #uuid "a54f7dfd-699f-5d20-82dd-a0e20cd154bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478052} {:answer/id #uuid "a68447ae-8b57-5690-8d88-53f52127910e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478055} {:answer/id #uuid "cddc4fa5-ede2-5454-8d85-97bd80342cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478144} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c133fae-f0bc-5d33-b131-23842ea13a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eac65ba4-c3fd-5292-817a-bbb6eafdc1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143379" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y{e}^{-3x},\\, & 0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2254d91b-2958-56f7-a120-27c4c74d4b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477802} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad94bc4-7977-503e-8e74-9824f2c3dd66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477884} {:answer/id #uuid "67b691a4-53c4-583b-a2ee-14c4b487753a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477944} {:answer/id #uuid "c3855371-283d-591c-8921-54d6156c7ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478123} {:answer/id #uuid "de2f4f3e-77a6-5b13-b71d-314248ef5d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477802} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18c8ecd-2d64-503b-a8ad-b4276195c72b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "faa5f199-79d7-5c93-85da-fb2ae026c8e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143582" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}, & -3\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ find $\\text{E}[3X+7Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+xy]}_{x=-3}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+3y)-(\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}-3y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[18+6y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3y+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{11}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+7Y] & =3\\,\\text{E}[X]+7\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3(0)+7\\left(\\frac{11}{21}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1370ffbd-2eab-5260-a0b4-0bf5d3cdb6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478582} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3f6cb5-53fa-5af5-81d3-830801107641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478750} {:answer/id #uuid "82d2a8f9-d415-5d71-a7d9-fe699cab072f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478758} {:answer/id #uuid "bedb09af-8e03-5cb2-a39f-8ac2f91ce3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} {:answer/id #uuid "e89460a9-557b-5055-9a71-6a052e322419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478867} :answer-field/id #uuid "aded51f8-58f0-5888-884c-244971621142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2317ff10-80a3-5747-858a-863cca47fbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152922" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480300} :answer-field/id #uuid "539ad74a-5113-59bb-99ce-f5b195bb6b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "967d4b0f-c5b8-576b-b414-bb183f8a1110" :question/math-academy-id "q-343368" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "857250c9-a9b1-5080-b2c5-1728296672ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480334} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e653195-c3d5-582e-822a-b0c443f4aade" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac1dfb2b-dc39-5a8f-bb78-6916e280fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343361" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ x+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=4 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-3)+{f}^{+}(-3)}{2}=\\frac{4+(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44247718-8f4f-58e6-895b-9f3dc89798eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481943} {:answer/id #uuid "89772cdb-fd83-51ae-aa1e-fed0f838a7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482093} {:answer/id #uuid "b36d7f94-7250-5b44-b690-333886d417f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482196} {:answer/id #uuid "db1bad53-8bf1-579a-bb9d-17ae6d5f1113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482295} {:answer/id #uuid "dba1842e-1014-5223-9a45-ba19604b7a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482295} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c59423-879c-5dcd-b08a-e948d0a753d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "049efa61-2f95-573c-8d4c-3a15cf157276" :question/math-academy-id "q-94705" :question/problem "Find the $90$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $90$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.9.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.9}) & ={\\int }_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}} & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}} & =0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{0.9} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cf873b-c561-529e-a150-dbd67081b794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,6,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481873} {:answer/id #uuid "2a39c9ca-304c-5dc6-bf0a-a3191df9d199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481882} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1d53b-065c-5f28-baee-103cc2d04fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481893} {:answer/id #uuid "6c32fa51-116b-5e6b-8ad8-9c15a7a0762b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482023} {:answer/id #uuid "c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482253} :answer-field/id #uuid "f864eeb7-5c3a-520c-b5c5-87f43508e40a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97023" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}, & 0\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 8$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(6-x)^{2}-2x(6-x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-x)(6-x-2x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128}\\,⟹\\,x=2,6.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,8],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,8.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,6,8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0(6-0)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2(6-2)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(6) & =\\frac{6(6-6)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(8) & =\\frac{8(6-8)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $2$ and $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3050f898-36aa-5645-a5a4-af8b4ba8fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481894} {:answer/id #uuid "92a7b198-9238-51a4-b8ab-d4d1a87d50ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482120} {:answer/id #uuid "ca571706-b58d-5784-b937-566421c78a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482255} {:answer/id #uuid "e38ccb96-44ea-55c6-99c9-dae1e3a87ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482315} {:answer/id #uuid "f88d6025-6a3c-5c70-abf4-3698342d3349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481894} :answer-field/id #uuid "37430a3d-0d71-5551-a19e-aa1fdf2b1819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07427b74-a9a0-5dbd-ab84-a95a81a836e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125516" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{195}+\\frac{38}{65}, & 2<x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $F(2)=\\frac{2}{3}>\\frac{1}{2},$ we have $0\\le m\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1138d2b7-a3e3-5532-97b5-c5fc9d0129c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481818} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1dae7a-d1c2-5268-ab76-bb5800e65796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481955} {:answer/id #uuid "62c8e9a7-f26a-5628-88e2-f271c7a23633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482001} {:answer/id #uuid "a48a09bd-cecc-5482-94cb-5f60175e9e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{32}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482164} {:answer/id #uuid "d675510a-f4a2-5e75-8380-eb715fc51139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481955} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8c632b-7961-5fad-8319-cad8210fb3f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26759ee4-9182-5088-bdc1-26b84fc02211" :question/math-academy-id "q-125510" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64},\\,0\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[3]{32}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4d79bc-915f-5faf-978f-4d42e48a1002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481886} {:answer/id #uuid "55c24ac5-9fd4-503b-ba36-10fa741c0864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481978} {:answer/id #uuid "78eefeb0-a4ae-5e62-bcd9-4a983105c3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482054} {:answer/id #uuid "793af677-077b-5623-8d55-c294d9651a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482055} {:answer/id #uuid "e345bb2f-b85f-50d4-b376-0cd925292cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482055} :answer-field/id #uuid "9860bd94-686f-562b-93e2-7506456e5e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b515338-9a49-5088-8d83-3ec16988abce" :question/math-academy-id "q-94641" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{-6y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}6{e}^{-6y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -6m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e85e52c-8e08-56c8-8b37-5f17ff7f2785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481808} {:answer/id #uuid "13548f7f-10aa-5faa-8652-5d6f71f80b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481827} {:answer/id #uuid "84c31569-5f15-5e6c-9553-68211785d8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482083} {:answer/id #uuid "c2733038-517d-5d08-9a0f-39b2b10dae78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482238} {:answer/id #uuid "c753b103-766c-5196-b73c-9776711e2c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482083} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10b6ffb-517f-5a7d-bbbf-4ef4514dfdb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f4c7c35-e12b-53f3-8c46-ab140e4e9992" :question/math-academy-id "q-125522" :question/problem "Find the $25$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1,\\, & 2<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $25$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.25.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.25}) & ={\\int }_{2}^{{x}_{0.25}}(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x)|}_{2}^{{x}_{0.25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $25$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}+1 & =0.25 \\\\ {x}_{0.25}^{2}-4{x}_{0.25}+3 & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{0.25}-3)({x}_{0.25}-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{0.25}=1,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${x}_{0.25}=3$ because $2<{x}_{0.25}<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "616aa6a5-4b43-534d-821a-bbdbc35c16f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481997} {:answer/id #uuid "8002f0e0-b8eb-52ab-98a1-fb52f05547d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482069} {:answer/id #uuid "803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482071} {:answer/id #uuid "91229bac-46a3-5f6c-aba9-119cb3512ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482116} {:answer/id #uuid "d675c4d0-7a79-5251-b122-3bcefc69e692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482071} :answer-field/id #uuid "89711e9c-e116-50a7-ae9d-6fbf9295b7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" :question/math-academy-id "q-97025" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}, & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{-(x+1)^{2}+2(2-x)(x+1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(-x-1+4-2x)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8}\\,⟹\\,x=-1,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-2,2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-2,-1,1,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\frac{(2+2)(-2+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(-1) & =\\frac{(2+1)(-1+1)^{2}}{8}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(2-1)(1+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{(2-2)(2+1)^{2}}{8}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-2$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab03d75-993a-5e56-a3b5-e9a96b0abe16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481793} {:answer/id #uuid "37007c7d-15c3-514b-a461-702523f7b376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481912} {:answer/id #uuid "8b123b93-545f-51fb-8532-62a1a090adcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482099} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cd0f4f-3e7c-5169-b1c0-6d7b3ad093ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482201} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cf6423-b887-5ec3-83c1-58b408697ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482201} :answer-field/id #uuid "21cbc6ee-ce3d-5b0e-a165-e71872b21032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "36945eec-4c74-5f74-8f42-dd9a1f9a922a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125520" :question/problem "Find the $51.2$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $51.2$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.512.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.512}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.512}}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{x}^{\\frac{3}{2}}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.512}} \\\\  & ={x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $51.2$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{3}{2}} & =0.512 \\\\ {x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{0.512} \\\\ {x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =0.8 \\\\ {x}_{0.512} & =(0.8)^{2} \\\\ {x}_{0.512} & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b1582a-fe2a-5af4-bba0-1ac6960815b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481874} {:answer/id #uuid "4485f3aa-42b6-5b6c-aa2f-0f673aee9fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481945} {:answer/id #uuid "955ec2a1-769b-5ec3-89e8-df042128fed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482126} {:answer/id #uuid "c16c04c9-0dbf-58fd-b26e-3de9324026c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482235} {:answer/id #uuid "c843aa7c-b566-56a4-85c9-ca8a883dcd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482252} :answer-field/id #uuid "27de064b-78af-5768-96b1-07df8a910cb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ca6810e-a219-54cd-a8c8-f575cfccdc55" :question/math-academy-id "q-125734" :question/problem "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<4, \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{q}_{1}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{16}{{q}_{1}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{q}_{3}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{16}{{q}_{3}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=8-\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0127daaf-87d8-5cdd-bdc6-723f2bd9ef53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481773} {:answer/id #uuid "5aec8f18-e1a4-58de-8c20-9808604c0760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481987} {:answer/id #uuid "a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482157} {:answer/id #uuid "ce66b1bb-2965-5a68-ba8d-76a5055610ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482263} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8f3418-48fb-5e2d-982c-77fd5497ed14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482157} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf31510e-14a1-5ed8-a2ac-a70c7049efd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426" :question/math-academy-id "q-126179" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{9}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=0$ is in the interval $[0,3],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {0}^{2}=0 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(3)=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd0b664-7cd0-5225-8077-d578f99f8c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481926} {:answer/id #uuid "5b668401-eb08-5c42-9289-5f7254bda777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481989} {:answer/id #uuid "857e38d3-3ddd-52a6-8559-6963c78e3a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482086} {:answer/id #uuid "93c6c840-9631-578b-8363-f6b9352ca751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482122} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4badf2-b2ff-5742-aa22-d9ace53e7565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482174} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2022eed-c29f-583a-a37e-b2d67f6aca04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "537b608f-d718-5a66-8b0b-7dabfac0375b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94670" :question/problem "Find the $21$st percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{21}x,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $21$st percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.21.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.21}) & ={\\int }_{2}^{{x}_{0.21}}\\frac{2}{21}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}|}_{2}^{{x}_{0.21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.21}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{4}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.21}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{4}{21} & =0.21 \\\\ {x}_{0.21}^{2} & =(0.21)(21)+4 \\\\ {x}_{0.21} & =2.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b5e394-80fe-57dd-9cb9-ba6e9edd273b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481825} {:answer/id #uuid "1befb3a2-1a61-55a8-b311-dd29e1df21d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481848} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6f4537-9695-5450-99bf-8967143319f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481887} {:answer/id #uuid "36753d1f-fd75-589a-8fb3-f329088220f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481910} {:answer/id #uuid "bd99344b-9ee9-5c83-8690-084c2fcd7f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481825} :answer-field/id #uuid "188532e0-67bb-558a-be7e-da0adb86d8b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "714df0ea-5d53-53df-b0aa-14ba037b9bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-94628" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{m} & =2 \\\\ m & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393f65ce-23ad-5614-aa05-25d2d89acbb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481916} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc505f2-78f1-5c70-88f4-d6032b9a5c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481927} {:answer/id #uuid "454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481946} {:answer/id #uuid "b01250a0-7f57-5f95-bfbf-f046438b94d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482190} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd25a84-48d7-5c69-a712-cc443b6851fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481946} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f670097-2378-5e08-98c6-e7db12d192b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95328" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,2.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfcbbaa-a8cc-51e4-be1a-c7f73905315a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481797} {:answer/id #uuid "74401d18-1e97-5151-b206-bf19cb24f8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{136}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482044} {:answer/id #uuid "9543e404-9adb-5088-a2f1-e2be2229a985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482125} {:answer/id #uuid "e4851740-2d3d-54dc-b48c-3c099aec6c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482317} {:answer/id #uuid "f028fb9b-aaf2-5ba7-81b1-86734cbf6171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[4]{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482044} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5b07f4-7bd6-5b7b-b298-c1c8eac0c1bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72db930f-dc67-5772-9a58-607de1ef27a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125515" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{240}({x}^{4}-16), & 2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16),\\,2\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[4]{136}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $2\\le m\\le 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211a57e2-d8aa-54b1-beea-511b212443ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.366" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481864} {:answer/id #uuid "59225a5a-4de1-5b47-a565-1141167812b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.666" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481983} {:answer/id #uuid "63d5bd69-c3d3-5125-9e45-bc46319d3648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482004} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ccde35-6145-571f-8819-7dd62e2850da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482160} {:answer/id #uuid "eb32e82a-d0cc-5c35-890f-63bb77ee3f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.636" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481864} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78defba-5c7e-59d1-ab03-e4daad3cbc4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e41b2e9-6ace-571f-a3ef-6f43b41df0ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-125712" :question/problem "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ because $0\\le {q}_{1}\\le 1.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ because $0\\le {q}_{3}\\le 1.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.366\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ccc100a-0804-57d3-934a-8576b15919de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481851} {:answer/id #uuid "5056f157-e7ac-5e18-b665-34631b5fa810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481968} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea8ecb1-b36f-55c1-97dd-23b6b06f366c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482030} {:answer/id #uuid "8d19a9c9-2f00-503d-a0ac-b3697e0af0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482105} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4067de-8e2f-50d3-a556-414aacb24659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482374} :answer-field/id #uuid "14f5578f-6447-52d0-b254-195bbd51df0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d19c0b1-13a1-5100-8957-4c982f273723" :question/math-academy-id "q-125773" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{98}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 7, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{7}^{m}\\frac{98}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{49}{{x}^{2}}|}_{7}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{{m}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{49}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{49}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m>7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1082a3b2-06e8-5365-82b5-32a2d22cab5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481815} {:answer/id #uuid "171293e6-ca8b-5e7f-9ecc-891ec0c09d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481840} {:answer/id #uuid "7442236d-8264-5a32-a3e2-b465b840d103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482045} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5d22cd-920b-5434-88ab-9c1bd372a226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482256} {:answer/id #uuid "d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482288} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d51fc38-d3aa-53d3-aca8-4f3a38a9dad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90f3bc1d-94f7-5716-a7c9-9b34d22c275c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126323" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=3{e}^{-3x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-9{e}^{-3x}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f.$ Therefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346a0350-155f-58bb-8439-0fb1c2e166bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481903} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb2dba4-a755-55b2-ae4f-bd6ef2d55129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481930} {:answer/id #uuid "433bcb79-f2e0-5f59-8bbf-c8a6fe6679ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481939} {:answer/id #uuid "5174e0ad-df19-5c17-a6e0-b9f1789a64e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481971} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc44424-4113-54de-a1c7-c3453bbbd004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481930} :answer-field/id #uuid "04eacb23-5651-500e-987d-0658add645b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a12ce635-7077-5ae6-9899-fd940aea64f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94601" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}, & 0\\le x\\le 12, \\\\ 1, & x>12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{144},\\,0\\le m\\le 12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{144}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=6\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b4ec8e-c3e5-5ae6-b5f1-963f8b25be11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.914" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481948} {:answer/id #uuid "498a13e5-8c2e-5df6-a277-b267feb9d7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.732" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481951} {:answer/id #uuid "eea8253c-9a91-5eee-8af4-be3818d42829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.646" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482345} {:answer/id #uuid "f0867a0c-eac5-5b6a-a170-f2f1b279db70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.914" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482349} {:answer/id #uuid "fa202739-d377-52d8-acd5-26cb18c3a306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.914" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481948} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ae13b6-0cd2-543d-bcb7-22657b86dffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4e71952-c18f-5e5a-8bbb-dfd0f7ad678f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125714" :question/problem "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8}, & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{1}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2}-1 & =2 \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ because $1\\le {q}_{1}\\le 3.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{3}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2}-1 & =6 \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =7 \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\sqrt{7}$ because $1\\le {q}_{3}\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & ≈0.914\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d9537c-4c6a-5bea-9084-7453673af462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481870} {:answer/id #uuid "363228b0-4656-5329-a192-45892c7d79d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481908} {:answer/id #uuid "5aeaf22f-9630-5a12-b24d-c6bcd8eefbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt[6]{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481986} {:answer/id #uuid "96e7c5e2-f3f8-5e51-b430-7a56684e572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482130} {:answer/id #uuid "9e404460-a0c2-5106-8aa8-4891726e0ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481908} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a4324b-f75b-536e-a236-773728d8dd8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6d47759-e842-511b-a810-c9880823e33f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94643" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{6}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =\\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082aacc4-affa-5540-8731-03749c801007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481787} {:answer/id #uuid "418a7860-4a6f-515d-a959-667a76f75c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481936} {:answer/id #uuid "95d04760-a4c8-5276-a4e8-6b9eb0e6763a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482127} {:answer/id #uuid "e34df759-bc0a-51b2-ad62-1b6a95b769d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482314} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0c571-503d-5fe4-884d-519e3a27a77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482314} :answer-field/id #uuid "af4d3735-879f-5545-9728-93257a5ef240" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80541" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and $y=3.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0b430b798a6dc85fd2b731a93690f79bb2c45b33bbe6f31a5618a2ce0888a.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156d8fb9-a3ef-5cf0-a116-cde0d34d5ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481835} {:answer/id #uuid "60d2f142-9346-53de-b0c8-157b88f8834d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481996} {:answer/id #uuid "961db6b5-e10b-5c17-9882-c4b693893797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482129} {:answer/id #uuid "b10db118-036b-5d52-8ae6-0ee7ed64170f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482191} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482264} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ca85d7-4841-50ea-a46d-18e2741e2de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126721" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+x)^{3}}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f(x).$\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5012aee4-8029-59e9-a001-1983600f8879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481966} {:answer/id #uuid "64c196ad-4c8a-588e-b2ff-6bdd2ba18aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482008} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9dbcca-797e-55ba-8abc-78fc021a5214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482033} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7b0abc-c581-56fb-bb65-2a52485c0bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482221} {:answer/id #uuid "bc448948-1a97-5631-ba43-c323b32dee1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482033} :answer-field/id #uuid "4273a9c4-b2af-5b72-ac14-00e304bf07b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc24bfb9-272f-54fa-b546-5cdc700f6030" :question/math-academy-id "q-94598" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25},\\,0\\le m\\le 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481980} {:answer/id #uuid "5eefcdf0-d372-5d82-907e-206da45b0d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481994} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb401a5-847f-54af-abdb-c557eab69963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482067} {:answer/id #uuid "88c4e86f-2e4e-50ce-94cf-69673f2e804b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482091} {:answer/id #uuid "aec77f18-46f9-56bc-9c5f-171f74af8827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481980} :answer-field/id #uuid "0846b1e1-5974-53e1-a637-306e6e3e8f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95380" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =10{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n10{x}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[-1,1],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-1,1.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-1,0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {0}^{4}}{2}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {1}^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb7b064-6ea7-5770-979d-8abdcb24a1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481810} {:answer/id #uuid "20ec2c09-e6fa-5afa-a648-fb02946dddda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481863} {:answer/id #uuid "58950bbd-e3ee-5c07-b30b-ddb473d62ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481982} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482205} {:answer/id #uuid "c74131b8-caab-5a3e-a0d9-32a36917054f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482205} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf38b3b7-889a-5d4b-add6-1de81acf50be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95348" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 5$ is $f(x)=\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=2$ is in the interval $[0,5],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,5.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 0-{0}^{2})=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2})=\\frac{27}{100} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{27}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040b0f42-7c9b-56eb-b6be-621b67822865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481778} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4b6a27-3956-5728-98e5-4951488d4928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481806} {:answer/id #uuid "16817ae5-5f38-5dd6-a4f9-252084ee3df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481837} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb42234-9f6f-50f6-b70b-14633db426c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481888} {:answer/id #uuid "d093b965-6d59-5136-ba07-f06c5e2040b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481837} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e63f712-8c7c-524e-90a0-bc57875864f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86895" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5f6dae762414eb327b9eae9e5445ef1916e9efc0e17c1316339bbeb416ec94e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ y-x, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R∖D, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/97/976dc1b0a7298c5cf0a331acb00594c8e4c74a14b6df3fc18f7e7997db2956c4.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y-x$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $y-x$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f35c0-9f74-51e8-b87c-18c9a10f1560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.855" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481777} {:answer/id #uuid "6b64a068-c831-5808-ab82-de97a974bd21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482020} {:answer/id #uuid "9b54931e-4f66-558e-ac52-6088a647f3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482142} {:answer/id #uuid "b12e960b-3cf9-57eb-a9a7-8546ad220ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.885" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482192} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e51d8b-3554-5198-9a2b-299876c96962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.854" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482020} :answer-field/id #uuid "21dc5a83-34c9-5825-801d-d29b40f9fa03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dfe1ae13-0566-5996-84a0-b50d07468365" :question/math-academy-id "q-94665" :question/problem "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{{q}_{1}}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{{q}_{1}}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{{q}_{3}}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{{q}_{3}}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}≈0.845\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d021253-7df2-5464-9178-35a626b44f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481890} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc863c8-ca1c-5f30-ab79-a7a81a707dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481961} {:answer/id #uuid "73a723a3-8cff-5a15-b741-6f6044bdef48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482043} {:answer/id #uuid "9cba564e-a6ed-532c-983c-2eea1eb2a4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482147} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d53989-5718-5924-bfd9-3c8e58a253e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482043} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29f424f-71fa-5ce6-a697-ab8b93c418a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f4cab15e-7525-590b-aa74-c49be38d3cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-94639" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}},\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{m}\\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}{|}_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {m}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ac1ac8-5d12-5b50-934c-de82720f574b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481839} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481844} {:answer/id #uuid "22e163e2-c51b-5f8c-acce-093bdea2aa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481868} {:answer/id #uuid "4dde1e24-43fb-5f71-b240-852e369c1dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0,1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481963} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cdcb5b-73ea-5836-bf70-b1a7b83f23d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481844} :answer-field/id #uuid "68323756-6915-5a2a-b83f-b1d14e9cdd18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127090" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2), & -1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{4}-2{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "708884fe-c46f-510c-8336-9b1ff8d57dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483787} {:answer/id #uuid "916cf1d3-9858-5e7a-bde5-ef0c01dfa40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483869} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7730f4-2296-5da8-a7ec-104cd0811268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483977} {:answer/id #uuid "de0ca903-22de-5647-90cb-d355eba5356b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484008} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bedab4-4484-581e-8c7f-86e59afba6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483787} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a9aeae-7475-58db-b6eb-574c342ffd6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "126e0a3b-1df5-561d-b629-66be47834dfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-90529" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}-{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206cf90d-4f29-55ab-9819-01d8ca172f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9e2311-3d05-5655-aec5-752bdfbf86b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483687} {:answer/id #uuid "7099a365-62d4-5172-aafe-41927474df4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483788} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf20927-1bb6-5558-9a95-f586e071ff7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483980} {:answer/id #uuid "db3da4ad-81ab-5111-a66e-0ed1cd8ee204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483687} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0bda9f-4431-5acd-981b-83f88e146a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ae79777-cfeb-50b2-91fa-571641f41141" :question/math-academy-id "q-90564" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1.9,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}, & 1\\le x<5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =5.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.5-{(1.9)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ba174f-6f4c-59fc-b3cb-137e1297aa50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483689} {:answer/id #uuid "554630d6-0001-5d34-98b7-ae518e793543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483721} {:answer/id #uuid "82de29c2-442b-50b3-b8a2-f2cef0536930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483833} {:answer/id #uuid "da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484000} {:answer/id #uuid "e46f85da-0699-50b2-9c52-f46103534b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484000} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143502" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66,$ calculate $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0606\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0594\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0582\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0735\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0721\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0708\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0885\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0853\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7088\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7123\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7157\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7422\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7454\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7486\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7764\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7794\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45})-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c298589-687a-53b7-a2f6-711c8b3fc022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483641} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed8eee1-36f5-552c-b95f-65eb970a734a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483685} {:answer/id #uuid "59e03d02-257a-5ba6-b196-538b39c66509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483734} {:answer/id #uuid "b279e53b-ef58-5c32-bfc7-abaacf3a829d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483923} {:answer/id #uuid "c5398c5f-3701-531a-9962-9d86c1e50901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483734} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc39d208-b352-55e6-ab15-3fec98ea96ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c369ac76-b620-5984-a1bf-927bb5bda8e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131261" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{8},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{3(x-1)}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3(x-1)}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3x-3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}+(-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}){|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+3-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14743a6d-89dc-51f1-be5f-651e9f9685f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483601} {:answer/id #uuid "b67a9aef-d76c-5bab-8625-14577b91fed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483932} {:answer/id #uuid "b98a4eda-a7fb-5b36-a2ee-9c2bf3e3cb46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483938} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f044e2-04da-5e9c-8032-d7a4b80cff3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483949} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c18450-3d1d-5233-9103-20c6853cd571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483601} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f8a025-5440-5c77-830a-6386ff67bfed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce424fe3-1289-5449-b3af-c9a36901436b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131166" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{x}^{3}},\\, & 1<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{9}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2b313e-b6a7-568c-a875-f9db4ce39dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483639} {:answer/id #uuid "325fa17b-54bd-5aa0-9e67-721dd15642fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483656} {:answer/id #uuid "5a881950-5b53-5733-8066-0e2b41f6af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483738} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4e985e-cf6f-56bf-aaa1-935086b1d3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483781} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f6ae5c-8838-54ee-bde6-979554af985b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483656} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6082ae9-a37f-5a6d-98af-d4b226bb7c29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fe9a9f2d-c658-56d3-a233-bcc579aed36f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107779" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=4$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9-x}{27}, & 3\\le x<9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{27}(9-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{1}{27}(9{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{54}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\frac{1}{27}(3{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e33eb3c-cec7-5493-9f32-c984010c4bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484871} {:answer/id #uuid "275b76d2-5fd6-553c-b3f2-cd785fa8f998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484889} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f8381-9c80-59cc-9eb2-c3583a9235be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} {:answer/id #uuid "c29e1a84-9ffd-505b-8a79-6dcd87d514f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4353" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485233} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea36b6-b0d0-5067-953e-f4ef81d92a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99fb244-28cb-58fa-857b-0ab86cacbcc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99aa1c5a-eed9-5ae6-b8c6-e2b13db7c0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96367" :question/problem "Mark runs a hotel near a popular tourist resort. He knows that, on average, people usually book their room $3.5$ days before their trip. By modeling the number of days between the customer's booking and arrival as continuous and assuming that these numbers are independent, determine the probability that the next customer books their room less than $1$ full day in advance. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time interval between the customer's booking and arrival is continuous and the time of different bookings are independent and occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time between the next customer's booking and arrival using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of days between a booking and the customer's arrival. Since customers book their room $3.5=7/2$ days in advance on average, $X$ can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/7,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer books a room less than $1$ full day in advance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/7}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/7}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2485\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54cf95-8d93-5f06-addc-038acebc5019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65b508-2845-571b-8454-76c6030fae62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2952" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484842} {:answer/id #uuid "48a6a32b-b9ab-5bec-85a1-8073a3608631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2529" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484974} {:answer/id #uuid "5814f8c1-9cae-53cc-8145-d58f8cc938cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485016} {:answer/id #uuid "668716e1-89c4-559b-bcb0-1a077d43cacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2f20a1-a54d-5ed7-a2a8-fb23d83de889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b831793d-1ec0-54c8-869c-6f86de445418" :question/math-academy-id "q-132546" :question/problem "Vehicles arrive at a crossing continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time intervals between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing are continuous and independent, and arrivals occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/10$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/10,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/10}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3/10} \\\\  & ≈0.2592\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04917684-3038-5e71-9f60-4e0cd96fb9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485775} {:answer/id #uuid "2fde4923-7da4-5a76-9103-bb5dec58b09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485855} {:answer/id #uuid "8db935a4-9e65-5534-8fcb-a44043a7dd25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486017} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2c9a38-93c6-5b27-b1f9-4d8c6ff8fdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486049} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2609b5-9caa-53f9-aea8-d0a896aed0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486217}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486217} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ad49b2-0cc7-539b-b32f-b7d844fbe92b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55430d88-2391-56d3-99b1-0b7835164965" :question/math-academy-id "q-143874" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{XY}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{XY}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe3c289-0f8e-54d7-ae79-90ed91af84a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=84,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485856} {:answer/id #uuid "6b18e2b2-d24f-565d-bc92-47ebd538d184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=81,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485948} {:answer/id #uuid "80d05a6e-4274-5fc6-8882-70b335cc878f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=84,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=36.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485992} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a24b85-d6ca-5625-8bec-1f5d00428da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=84,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=33.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486123} {:answer/id #uuid "e64e2775-6379-524b-b45e-66d7d130796f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[X]=81,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=33.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486123} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4f1e19-3dfb-5e8c-a60d-6c3ee527f11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6975e0db-6a89-53e9-8d9a-8975ae20b85d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146403" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60\\%=0.6,\\,n=140.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(140,0.6)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =84 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.6\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =33.6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229eafe1-24fb-552a-9117-a7fa14447b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485822} {:answer/id #uuid "55f3e41b-c1ec-5a55-bfc6-d4f47c03e528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{363}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485916} {:answer/id #uuid "77fbb658-cabf-571c-998f-24429c73e55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485974} {:answer/id #uuid "881a60af-adf5-5e87-afec-cbdae3c947af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486004} {:answer/id #uuid "e5aa2ec3-55fd-5125-8765-b0a5ef54e5b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{363}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485974} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4dfdca8-bd20-538c-8d34-664a31d6e354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f9d70b5-8ba3-5fd2-851b-442924453b0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143891" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[X+3{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{15},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{x}{3}\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{x}{3}\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/bc/bc454de7a5ef0a9b0e2620bb44a0d0b481638ca04905bf6d986a52715a95c3db.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+3{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(x+3{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x/3}^{3}(x+3{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{15}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=x/3}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[3x+27-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+27x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{108}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}[\\frac{27}{2}+81-3-\\frac{3}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{363}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d62574-5135-5ee2-98ac-fbd52149ff4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485802} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8fc363-c3d7-5b52-8ce7-0c5155a2ede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{3}-{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485881} {:answer/id #uuid "4c27f43c-7bf7-5350-bb36-6f94522b814a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+2{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485904} {:answer/id #uuid "66876a52-b756-5569-ad24-5c61115dd2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485939} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a0be36-7fd5-5956-bc6c-39bb19ba46df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{3}-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485881} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8d8e35-f3a0-568a-a543-4b0ff0ce195c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6992831-85a4-570c-8e6c-b6898df7292a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143834" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3xy},\\, & 1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/09/09bf58b8505f860709d705f128b2bafbd6916382de56ba66d26a1a56319f0141.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{y}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{3xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}[x]_{x=1}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}(y-1)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]}_{y=e}^{y={e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[(\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}-{e}^{2})-(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2}+e) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{3}-{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6e9aa3-7579-5a88-aab9-009a45405b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.42)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485844} {:answer/id #uuid "4d29a851-eb8b-501c-abce-b34ca4a45796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(66,0.42)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485907} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfb1579-730f-5017-931e-2e7002367e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(28,0.42)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486015} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bcdc0f-1389-5e8c-a2f7-0ffa32acc877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B(66,0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486083} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae63a2-5d82-5b0e-b38b-f7334456bc5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485907} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b6f92b-32ff-54a7-9dff-a149716ae98e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bfc489e6-3fde-599d-8b94-fa54c6ab85f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-146400" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=66$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,66.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{66}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=42\\%=0.42,\\,n=66.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(66,0.42).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ec1015-67ae-532c-90ae-ecdaface5f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.4,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485960} {:answer/id #uuid "a2dbf744-dd49-5c6d-a61b-984a7259504c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486057} {:answer/id #uuid "adf531a6-7319-5b80-9274-0509621d73e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486077} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4631fc-533d-5b33-96dd-c7fb0f7aac14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.4,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486133} {:answer/id #uuid "fd23b3bb-07c4-5f49-97bc-5ab8e4e8d978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486227} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c7b60b-ebc0-552f-bb22-591e7a44c23f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1024622-b9b9-5914-883d-61f4135d4db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-146442" :question/problem "Suppose that $60\\%$ of the child population enrolled in a baseball academy is right-handed. A random sample of $n=20$ children is taken from the population and asked about their dominant hand.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of right-handed children in the sample? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=60\\%=0.6,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =60\\%=0.6, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{20} \\\\  & =0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
